.\" Manual page for pfadi.
.\" Checked against Invocation.helpText by the self-test: every option named in
.\" one has to appear in the other, so this cannot quietly go out of date.
.Dd August 7, 2026
.Dt PFADI 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pfadi
.Nd a file browser with an address bar you can click into and type
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar folder ...
.Nm
.Fl R Ar path
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl -help | Fl v | Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
opens a window on a folder.
It exists because macOS has no address bar you can click into and type: the
nearest thing is a dialog behind
.Ic \(**\(uAG ,
which forgets what you typed the moment it closes.
.Pp
With no arguments it opens the folder the shell is in.
The command returns as soon as the window has been asked for, so it can be put
at the end of a line without holding the terminal.
.Pp
The arguments are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar folder
Open it.
Several folders open as tabs of one window rather than as separate windows.
.It Ar file
Open the folder holding it, with the file selected.
.It Fl R , Fl -reveal Ar path
Select
.Ar path
in the folder holding it, even when
.Ar path
is itself a folder.
Without this, a folder given as an argument is opened rather than pointed at.
.It Fl -
Everything after this is a path, not an option.
A folder really can be called
.Pa --help .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl v , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev PFADI_APP
The application bundle to open.
Set this when running from a build directory rather than an install.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A path that does not exist, an unknown option, or a missing application bundle
are all reported on standard error and exit 1.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Open the current folder:
.Pp
.Dl $ pfadi
.Pp
Open two folders as tabs of one window:
.Pp
.Dl $ pfadi ~/git ~/Downloads
.Pp
Point at a file rather than opening it:
.Pp
.Dl $ pfadi -R ./report.pdf
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr open 1 ,
.Xr pfadi-default 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Sebastian Winterberger
.Aq Mt sapn95@users.noreply.github.com
.Sh COLUMNS
Twelve, of which
.Sy Name , Sy Size No and Sy Modified
are on to begin with.
Right-click the column headers for the rest, or use
.Sy View No \(en Sy Columns .
Drag a header to move a column.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "Permissions" -compact
.It Sy Name
Cannot be switched off.
.It Sy Size
Files from the filesystem, folders by walking them.
.It Sy Files
How many are inside, from that same walk.
.It Sy Modified , Sy Created , Sy Added , Sy Last Opened
.Sy Added
is when it arrived in this folder, which in a downloads folder is the only date
that means anything.
.It Sy Kind , Sy Extension , Sy Tags
.It Sy Permissions , Sy Owner
Read with
.Xr lstat 2
rather than through the resource keys, which follow symbolic links: a link
shows as
.Sy l...\&
here, which is the useful answer.
.El
.Pp
Only the columns on screen are read off the disk, plus whichever column is being
sorted by even when it is hidden.
Every header sorts except
.Sy Files ,
whose number arrives after the listing does.
.Sh CAVEATS
Folder sizes in the size column are measured by walking the folder, because the
filesystem does not record them.
Only the rows on screen are measured, and a walk that reaches its limit is shown
as
.Dq over
a number rather than as a total.
Sorting by that column measures every folder in the listing instead, because an
order worked out from the rows that happen to be visible is not an order.
.Pp
The folders macOS keeps inside a home directory
.Pq Pa ~/Documents , Pa ~/Desktop , Pa ~/Library No and their kind
cannot be moved to the trash.
The system refuses by returning success and doing nothing, so every trash is
checked afterwards rather than believed, and a selection containing one of them
still trashes everything else.
